+## Checksum-Compatible Resume Projection Integrity
+
+A run whose workflow source still has the same root checksum can nonetheless persist identities the current program no longer owns. Resume therefore audits every scope it will execute against that scope's current projection and fails closed before any mutation or effect. The state schema stays `"2.1"`; pre-v2.0 state and pre-2.1 reusable-call state remain rejected. The audit reads no procedure-migration, identity-retirement or other migration evidence, keeps no receipts and no persisted audit cache, and never mutates the state it audits.
+
+Scope audit (`orchestrator.workflow.resume_projection_integrity.audit_scope(bundle, state, scope_path)`):
+- Reads only the bundle and state it is given: it loads no workflow or bundle, computes no checksum and reads no file.
+- Every present explicit durable step identity (`steps.<name>.step_id`, `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`) must resolve to exactly one candidate owned by the audited scope's projection. Qualified loop and call identities resolve only through projection-owned candidates; resume never splits, normalizes, prefix-matches, reconstructs, remaps or backfills ids and never selects by workflow, module or procedure name, basename, path suffix or persisted alias.
+- An absent `steps.<name>.step_id` stays compatible for every schema-valid row the presentation/name/order fallback supports, whatever its status; resume leaves the field absent. A present `step_id` is always audited. `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.*.call_step_id`, a call frame's current position and loop progress selectors remain mandatory.
+- `for_each` bookkeeping: `items` indices are unique non-boolean non-negative integers; the optional `current_index` is in range and not completed. `repeat_until` admits exactly four consistent progress forms: active; terminal condition success; terminal successful exhaustion (`last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted: true`, a completed loop step); terminal failed exhaustion (`last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted` absent or false, a failed loop step whose `error.type` is `repeat_until_iterations_exhausted`). Both exhaustion forms cover the declared maximum range; every terminal form has `current_iteration: null` and identifies the terminal completed/evaluated iteration. Valid bookkeeping does not excuse a stale or out-of-scope loop-local step or call-boundary id.
+- Every call frame validates caller identity, import alias, boundary ownership and status. Completed frames are unlimited historical records and never resumable. A boundary whose callee is not Workflow Lisp (typed `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"`, never a `.orc` suffix or a name) has at most one non-completed frame. A Workflow Lisp boundary's non-completed frames form one retry lineage: base `B` (ordinal 0) and retries `B::retry::<n>`, with zero or one running member and any number of failed predecessors; mixed bases, several running members, a duplicate ordinal, a malformed or nested retry marker, an unknown status, or a caller/alias differing from the boundary's fail closed. The next fresh retry id is `B::retry::<lowest unused positive ordinal over all members, completed included>`.
+
+Placement and ordering:
+1. After schema and root-checksum acceptance, `orchestrator resume` audits the persisted root scope (`audit_scope` in `orchestrator.cli.commands.resume`) before observability overrides, executor session or process metadata, executor construction and the execution prologue. A failure exits 1, prints the reason to standard error and persists only the failure envelope below. The initial CLI root-checksum precheck keeps its behaviour (exit 1, nothing written).
+2. A `WorkflowExecutor` that resumes as the structural root rechecks the authoritative checksum (through `StateManager.calculate_checksum`) and then audits the root scope immediately before its prologue. A child call-frame executor does not repeat the root guard.
+3. A reached call during resume revalidates frame shape, caller boundary, persisted import alias and lineage. It resolves its persisted caller identity against the current parent projection with one `WorkflowStateProjection.resolve_call_boundary(slot_index, call_step_id)` per reached call identity; exactly one current boundary must own it and that boundary's current import alias selects the callee bundle (the persisted alias is checked, never used to redirect). Callee validation happens after the parent call visit/start publication and after the call's authored-input, managed/runtime-input, write-root, checksum and applicable resume-bound-input validation, but before the callee's frame or state is created or changed and before any callee effect; nested scopes apply the rule recursively. With a running lineage member its checksum and resume-bound-input validation run first and win on failure; then every failed predecessor is checksum-validated and recursively audited in ordinal order; then the running member's scope is audited and resumed. Without a running member every failed predecessor is checksum-validated and audited before the fresh retry frame is allocated. A completed frame whose id collides with the frame a resumed call would occupy is rejected before lineage grouping with `contract_violation` / `call_resume_state_invalid` / `completed_call_frame_id_collision`.
+
+Failure envelopes (only root `status`, `error` and `updated_at` change; unknown root fields and the run tree are preserved):
+- A structurally root executor whose checksum recheck fails persists `error = {"type": "workflow_checksum_mismatch", "message": "Workflow has been modified since the run started", "context": {"workflow_file", "persisted_checksum", "current_checksum", "reason"}}` with `reason` one of `workflow_modified`, `missing_recorded_checksum`, `missing_workflow_path`, `workflow_unavailable`, and stops before the projection audit and the prologue (`StateManager.record_workflow_checksum_mismatch`).
